{"name":"Camel Spider","hex":"#af8751","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"camel spider","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any fast-moving, predatory desert arachnid belonging to the order Solifugae having massive paired chelicerae and a long body covered with tactile hairs."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/camel_spider"}],"entries":[{"title":"Solifugae","blocks":[{"text":"Solifugae is an order of arachnids known variously as solifuges, sun spiders, camel spiders, wind scorpions, and jerrymanders. The order includes more than 1,000 described species in about 147 genera. Despite their common names, they differ from both order Araneae (spiders) and order Scorpiones (scorpions). Most species of solifuges live in dry climates and feed opportunistically on ground-dwelling arthropods and other small animals. The largest species grow to a length of 12–15 cm (5–6 in), including legs. A number of urban legends exaggerate the size and speed of solifuges, and their potential danger to humans, which is negligible."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/Solifuge_on_white_background%2C_2026-09-28.jpg/330px-Solifuge_on_white_background%2C_2026-09-28.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Solifugae","wikidata":"Q19126"},{"title":"Camel","blocks":[{"text":"A camel is an even-toed ungulate in the genus Camelus that bears distinctive fatty deposits known as \"humps\" on its back. Camels have long been domesticated and, as livestock, they provide food and textiles. Camels are working animals especially suited to their desert habitat and are a vital means of transport for passengers and cargo. There are three surviving species of camel. The one-humped dromedary makes up 94% of the world's camel population, and the two-humped Bactrian camel makes up 6%. The wild Bactrian camel is a distinct species that is not ancestral to the domestic Bactrian camel, and is now endangered, with around 1,000 individuals and a continuing decline."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/07._Camel_Profile%2C_near_Silverton%2C_NSW%2C_07.07.2007.jpg/330px-07._Camel_Profile%2C_near_Silverton%2C_NSW%2C_07.07.2007.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Camel","wikidata":"Q7375"},{"title":"Spider","blocks":[{"text":"Spiders are air-breathing arthropods that have eight limbs, chelicerae with fangs generally able to inject venom, and spinnerets that extrude silk. They are the largest order of arachnids and rank seventh in total species diversity among all orders of organisms. Spiders are found worldwide on every continent except Antarctica, and have become established in nearly every land habitat. As of January 2026, 53,680 spider species in 139 families have been recorded by taxonomists. However, there has been debate among scientists about how families should be classified, with over 20 different classifications proposed since 1900."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/Araneus_diadematus_%28Clerck%2C_1757%29.JPG/330px-Araneus_diadematus_%28Clerck%2C_1757%29.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spider","wikidata":"Q1357"},{"title":"Camel (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Camel is a color that resembles the color of the hair of a camel."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Camelcalf-feeding.jpg/330px-Camelcalf-feeding.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Camel_(color)","wikidata":"Q17082553"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/20220608_Solifugae_morphology.png/500px-20220608_Solifugae_morphology.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/20220608_Solifugae_morphology.png/1280px-20220608_Solifugae_morphology.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/20220608_Solifugae_morphology.png/500px-20220608_Solifugae_morphology.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Generalized solifugid anatomy[a] ","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A20220608_Solifugae_morphology.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Solpugidae_showing_eyes_with_presumably_protective_bristles_2012_01_24_1019s.JPG/500px-Solpugidae_showing_eyes_with_presumably_protective_bristles_2012_01_24_1019s.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Solpugidae_showing_eyes_with_presumably_protective_bristles_2012_01_24_1019s.JPG/1280px-Solpugidae_showing_eyes_with_presumably_protective_bristles_2012_01_24_1019s.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Solpugidae_showing_eyes_with_presumably_protective_bristles_2012_01_24_1019s.JPG/500px-Solpugidae_showing_eyes_with_presumably_protective_bristles_2012_01_24_1019s.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Solifuge eyes with bristly setae","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASolpugidae_showing_eyes_with_presumably_protective_bristles_2012_01_24_1019s.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/Solfugid_in_veld_near_Uniondale_%28Western_Cape%29_1600.jpg/500px-Solfugid_in_veld_near_Uniondale_%28Western_Cape%29_1600.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/Solfugid_in_veld_near_Uniondale_%28Western_Cape%29_1600.jpg/1280px-So